[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Handling Concurrency in .NET: Optimistic vs. Pessimistic Locking"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building multi-tier or web-scale applications in .NET, multiple users or processes frequently attempt to update the same data concurrently. Without proper concurrency control, this leads to race conditions, lost updates, and data corruption. Entity Framework (EF) Core provides two primary strategies to handle this: Optimistic Concurrency and Pessimistic Concurrency."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimistic Concurrency assumes that data conflicts are rare. It allows transactions to proceed without locking database rows, checking if another transaction modified the data just before committing."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pessimistic Concurrency assumes conflicts are likely. It locks the database records immediately upon reading them, preventing other transactions from reading or writing until the lock is released."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Optimistic Concurrency in EF Core"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The standard approach for optimistic concurrency in EF Core involves using a concurrency token, commonly a timestamp or a row version property. When the database updates the row, it checks if the token matches the value originally read."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public class Product
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public decimal Price { get; set; }

    [Timestamp]
    public byte[] RowVersion { get; set; }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a concurrent modification occurs, EF Core throws a DbUpdateConcurrencyException duringSaveChanges or SaveChangesAsync. You must handle this exception to resolve the conflict."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"try
{
    await context.SaveChangesAsync();
}
catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException ex)
{
    foreach (var entry in ex.Entries)
    {
        var databaseEntry = await entry.GetDatabaseValuesAsync();
        if (databaseEntry == null)
        {
            // The entity was deleted by another user
        }
        else
        {
            var databaseValues = (Product)databaseEntry.ToObject();
            var clientValues = (Product)entry.Entity;

            // Restoring original values or merging changes
            entry.OriginalValues.SetValues(databaseEntry);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Pessimistic Concurrency"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pessimistic concurrency relies on database locks (such as SELECT ... FOR UPDATE). EF Core does not have a built-in API for pessimistic locking, so you must use raw SQL queries via FromSqlRaw."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"var product = await context.Products
    .FromSqlRaw("SELECT * FROM Products WITH (UPDLOCK, ROWLOCK) WHERE Id = {0}", productId)
    .SingleOrDefaultAsync();

product.Price = 150.00m;
await context.SaveChangesAsync();"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary: When to Use Which?"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Optimistic Concurrency for web applications, APIs, and scenarios with high read-to-write ratios to maximize throughput and scalability."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Pessimistic Concurrency for financial transactions, inventory management, or high-contention batch processes where data integrity strictly overrides concurrency performance."}]
